+\subsection{Research Track: Planck-Scale Route Audit as a Single Algebraic Seed}
+\label{subsec:rt_planck_routes_single_seed_audit}
+
+\paragraph{Status.}
+\textbf{[RESEARCH-TRACK] [TRIAL] [NOT DERIVED] [FITTED].}
+This subsection replaces the stronger four-route interpretation by a stricter
+audit statement.  The four candidate routes below are not independent
+confirmations.  They are algebraic representations of one seed relation once the
+canonical Compton--core closure and horn-density closure are used.  Therefore
+the shared residual is not evidence for four convergent derivations; it is the
+same residual written in four variables.
+
+\paragraph{Single seed relation.}
+The common seed candidate may be written as
+\begin{align}
+    G_{\star}
+    &=
+    \frac{\pi^3}{16}\,
+    \frac{\rho_{\!f}\,\vchar^{9}\,r_c^4}{M_e^2 c^7},
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_seed_G}\
+    &=
+    \frac{\pi^3}{2^{17}}\,
+    \alpha_{\rm SST}^{13}\,
+    \frac{\rho_{\!f}\,\hbar^4}{M_e^6c^2},
+    \qquad
+    \alpha_{\rm SST}\equiv\frac{2\vchar}{c},
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_seed_alpha_form}
+\end{align}
+where the second form uses the Compton--core closure
+\begin{align}
+    \hbar
+    \simeq
+    \frac{M_ec^2r_c}{\vchar}.
+\end{align}
+With the current numerical constants this gives
+\begin{align}
+    G_{\star}
+    &=
+    6.6361520\times10^{-11}\,
+    \mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}},
+    \\
+    \frac{G_{\star}}{G_N}
+    &=
+    0.99428435,
+    \\
+    t_{p,\star}
+    &=
+    \sqrt{\frac{\hbar G_{\star}}{c^5}}
+    =
+    5.3758171\times10^{-44}\,\mathrm{s},
+    \\
+    \frac{t_{p,\star}}{t_p}
+    &=
+    0.99713808.
+\end{align}
+This numerical proximity is a research target only.  It is not a derivation of
+\(G\), \(L_p\), or \(t_p\).
+
+\paragraph{Four route representations of the same seed.}
+The line-density representation is
+\begin{align}
+    \left(\sigma_{\rm pierce}\Lambda_L\right)_{A}
+    =
+    \frac{16}{\pi^2}\,
+    \frac{\rho_{\rm core}}{\rho_{\!f}}
+    \left(\frac{c}{\vchar}\right)^6
+    \frac{1}{r_c^2} .
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_route_A_single_seed}
+\end{align}
+The induced-mode representation is exactly
+\begin{align}
+    N_B
+    =
+    2r_c^2
+    \left(\sigma_{\rm pierce}\Lambda_L\right)_{A}
+    =
+    \frac{32}{\pi^2}\,
+    \frac{\rho_{\rm core}}{\rho_{\!f}}
+    \left(\frac{c}{\vchar}\right)^6 .
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_route_B_single_seed}
+\end{align}
+The pressure-susceptibility representation is
+\begin{align}
+    G_C
+    =
+    \frac{\pi^2}{32}\,
+    \frac{\rho_{\!f}}{\rho_{\rm core}}
+    \left(\frac{\vchar}{c}\right)^5
+    \frac{\vchar^2r_c}{M_e} .
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_route_C_single_seed}
+\end{align}
+The maximum-tension representation is its reciprocal force-bound form,
+\begin{align}
+    F_D
+    =
+    \frac{c^4}{4G_C}.
+    \label{eq:rt_planck_route_D_single_seed}
+\end{align}
+Equivalently, using the horn-density closure to eliminate \(G_C\),
+\begin{align}
+    F_D
+    =
+    \frac{16}{\pi^2}\,
+    F_{\rm swirl}^{\max}
+    \frac{\rho_{\rm core}}{\rho_{\!f}}
+    \left(\frac{c}{\vchar}\right)^7,
+\end{align}
+up to the rounding used in the tabulated value of \(F_{\rm swirl}^{\max}\).
+
+\paragraph{Equivalence lemma.}
+Under the closures
+\begin{align}
+    \hbar\simeq\frac{M_ec^2r_c}{\vchar},
+    \qquad
+    \rho_{\rm core}\simeq
+    \frac{M_ec^2}{2\pi\vchar^2r_c^3},
+\end{align}
+one obtains the identities
+\begin{align}
+    N_B
+    &=
+    2r_c^2
+    \left(\sigma_{\rm pierce}\Lambda_L\right)_A,
+    \\
+    2\left(\sigma_{\rm pierce}\Lambda_L\right)_A
+    \frac{\hbar G_C}{c^3}
+    &\simeq
+    1,
+    \\
+    F_D
+    &=
+    \frac{c^4}{4G_C},
+    \\
+    G_C
+    &\simeq
+    G_{\star}.
+\end{align}
+Thus the four equations are one trial seed written as horizon piercing density,
+induced mode count, pressure susceptibility, and maximum tension.
+
+\paragraph{Look-elsewhere disclosure.}
+The low-complexity scan used the family
+\begin{align}
+    G
+    =
+    G_0
+    \left(\frac{\rho_{\!f}}{\rho_{\rm core}}\right)^k
+    \left(\frac{\vchar}{c}\right)^n
+    \pi^p2^m,
+    \qquad
+    G_0=\frac{\vchar^2r_c}{M_e},
+\end{align}
+with
+\begin{align}
+    k\in[-3,3],\quad
+    n\in[-20,20],\quad
+    p\in[-8,8],\quad
+    m\in[-12,12].
+\end{align}
+This is a grid of \(121{,}975\) candidates.  In that grid, \(31\) candidates
+fall within \(5\%\) of \(G_N\), and \(5\) candidates fall within
+\(0.575\%\).  A better numerical hit exists in the same grid with
+\((k,n,p,m)=(0,15,-6,7)\), i.e. without the density ratio.  Therefore the
+sub-percent match of Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_planck_seed_G} has no evidentiary force
+without an independent derivation of the exponent structure and kernel.
+
+\paragraph{Degeneracy disclosure.}
+Exact closure of Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_planck_seed_G} to \(G_N\) would require
+\begin{align}
+    \rho_{\!f}^{\rm exact}
+    =
+    7.04024\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}},
+\end{align}
+whereas the current canonical value is rounded as
+\(\rho_{\!f}=7.0\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}\).  Moreover,
+Eq.~\eqref{eq:rt_planck_seed_alpha_form} scales as
+\begin{align}
+    \frac{\partial\ln G_{\star}}{\partial\ln\alpha_{\rm SST}}=13,
+\end{align}
+so a fractional shift of order \(4.4\times10^{-4}\) in the effective
+\(\vchar/c\) calibration absorbs the residual.  The residual is therefore
+degenerate among \(\rho_{\!f}\), the effective \(\alpha_{\rm SST}\), and the
+fitted angular/kernel factor.
+
+\paragraph{Canonization gate.}
+The useful content of this audit is not a Planck-time derivation.  The useful
+content is the sharp research target:
+\begin{align}
+    \sigma_{\rm pierce}\Lambda_L
+    \stackrel{?}{=}
+    \frac{1}{2L_p^2}
+    =
+    1.9140\times10^{69}\,\mathrm{m^{-2}},
+\end{align}
+with the requirement that \(\Lambda_L\) and \(\sigma_{\rm pierce}\) be derived
+from SST vacuum-tangle statistics without using \(G\), \(L_p\), or \(t_p\) as
+inputs.  A natural next target is an Onsager/Kosterlitz--Thouless-type
+vortex-tangle derivation of the vacuum line density.
